## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: d37442cdb1189c7202c177901832e34f582e2feb

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** I traced the history of the affected line in `Minecraft.java` around the pause-on-Escape logic using `git log -p -S "isLoadingLevel"`. This showed the exact condition `if (!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel){ pause(); } ...` was introduced in commit `d37442c` ("Fixes #319"), and was preceded/followed by a related commit `754e965` ("Fixed bug in last commit") that tweaked a *different* branch of the same diff. I then checked `PacketHandler.java` to understand `isLoadingLevel`'s lifecycle.

**(2) Specific change:** Commit `d37442c` changed the Escape-key handler from unconditionally calling `pause()` to:
```java
if (!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel){
    pause();
} else {
    Mouse.setGrabbed(false);
}
```
This was meant to fix a multiplayer loading-screen flicker (issue #319), but it didn't account for singleplayer.

**(3) Supporting evidence:** In `PacketHandler.java`, the constructor unconditionally does `setLoadingLevel(true)`, and `isLoadingLevel` is only ever set back to `false` in response to network packets (level-loading protocol from a server). In singleplayer there is no network handshake, so `packetHandler.isLoadingLevel` never transitions to `false` — it stays `true` for the entire singleplayer session. Consequently, after `d37442c`'s change, `!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel` is always `false` in singleplayer, so `pause()` (which opens the pause menu) is never invoked when Escape is pressed — exactly the "menu doesn't show up in singleplayer" symptom described in the fix commit `c34d7ad`, which patches it by adding `|| !isOnline()` to explicitly bypass the loading check when not connected to a server.

The intermediate commit `754e965` only adjusted the *other* branch of the same diff (the keyboard-handling loop that runs *while* `isLoadingLevel` is true, unrelated to the `currentScreen == null` pause logic that `c34d7ad` fixes), so it did not address — and is not responsible for — this particular singleplayer regression.

I'm confident in this identification: the code evidence (constructor default `true`, and no path to set it `false` outside network packet handling) directly explains the symptom fixed by `c34d7ad`.

